{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_l24nfm4rw2wwnpfnbbi7junn5m7pzfqwdc6p34n4jrdqsylofzhq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Here's what I found and fixed.\n\n## Surface (Phase A)\n\nThe project is a single 45-line C translation unit with three functions (`print_hex` static, `driver`, `main`). Built as a `.so` it exports exactly **`driver`** and **`main`**. `Cargo.toml` declares **no `[features]`**, so the feature power-set is a single element (verified mechanically by `check_features.sh`, which would expand the full power-set if any feature existed).\n\nArtifacts created: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (21 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (35 runtime rows + 5 build rows), `NOTES.md`.\n\n## Structural fix\n\nThe crate was bin-only, so it exported **nothing**. I added a `cdylib` lib target (`src/lib.rs`) with `#[no_mangle] extern \"C\"` wrappers for `driver` and `main`, sharing one implementation (`src/logic.rs`) with the bin. Symbol diff against the C `.so` is now empty in both directions, with no undefined non-libc symbols.\n\n## Real behavioral divergences found and fixed\n\nThe original translation's stdout/parse logic was already value-correct, but its stdin handling was not:\n\n1. **Buffered `StdinLock` (8 KiB) over-read the descriptor.** The C program reads stdin through glibc's `FILE`, so I reproduced that exactly: one process-wide buffer, size `st_blksize` when `0 < st_blksize < BUFSIZ` else `BUFSIZ`, one `read(2)` per refill, no `EINTR` retry, sticky EOF (`_IO_EOF_SEEN`) but **non**-sticky read error.\n2. **Missing exit-time `_IO_cleanup` seek-back.** C seeks stdin back over unconsumed buffered bytes at exit (`ESPIPE` ignored). Before: `{ ./driver; cat; } < file` printed `rest-of-file` for Rust vs ` rest-of-file` for C; a 20 002-byte pipe left 0 bytes vs C's 15 906. Now identical.\n3. **No shared stdin state across calls.** Calling the `.so`'s `main` four times on `1 2 3` gave C `01,02,03,00` but Rust `01,00,00,00`. Now identical — independently re-confirmed with a separate Python/`ctypes` harness.\n\n## Tests (all through `dlopen`, never calling Rust directly)\n\n58 tests in 7 files: `driver` (byte-class sweeps, single-bit, boundaries, 20 000 random `i32`, garbage in the upper half of the argument register), `main` (whitespace classes, signs, terminators, digit-run lengths, `long`/`int` saturation and truncation, 4096-byte buffer straddling, pipe/`/dev/null`/closed/directory fd 0, random blobs), descriptor-state rows, end-to-end executable comparison, one test per `ERRORS.md` row with the C output anchored explicitly, and `nm -D` parity. Plus an `#[ignore]`d 30 s soak (200 000 `driver` values, 20 000 stdin blobs, digit lengths 1–400).\n\n`./run_all.sh` from a clean tree: **ALL CHECKS PASSED** — every feature invocation × dev/release profile, plus symbol parity. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified (only the sanctioned `c_src/build/` output dir).\n\nTwo limits are documented rather than \"fixed\", since they are properties of libc's global `FILE` objects that no non-libc translation can share: a host process that has already driven glibc's `stdin` to EOF, and the fact that C's `printf` output sits in libc's buffer until exit (which is why the harness flushes the C side, and why leftover stdin bytes are reported before that flush — `fflush(NULL)` also syncs input streams and would measure the harness instead of the implementation)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_read_input_tokens":203572,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":693,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7fe536a-6767-4181-b2b8-ac996b1436df","uuid":"1f1e1971-1dcf-488e-8a84-25cfb19d4756","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:50:50.581Z"}
